WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.280
Auditory training develops the child's use of available sound.

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:11.540
A hearing aid or cochlear implant can supply access, but the child still learns to notice, attend to, and interpret the signal.

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:13.430
Start with assessment.

00:00:14.285 --> 00:00:22.285
Determine whether training is needed, establish a baseline for later comparison, and identify the specific skills to target.

00:00:22.605 --> 00:00:29.645
For an infant, observations and caregiver reports can be more meaningful than a word test the child cannot yet perform.

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:33.530
Keep device function and audiologic follow-up in that picture.

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:39.510
Before choosing an auditory-only contrast, confirm that the child can actually access it.

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:44.060
Repeating an inaudible sound does not solve the access problem.

00:00:44.840 --> 00:00:48.920
Then involve caregivers in purposeful daily routines.

00:00:49.605 --> 00:00:56.965
A structured clinic activity and a listening opportunity during dressing can support the same goal when the response and conditions are clear.

